[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't define it there, but you see it in other places in the suttas.

21
00:02:34,236 --> 00:02:40,919
[SPEAKER_00]: It defines it as basically any kind of displeasure out of...

22
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:47,521
[SPEAKER_00]: It says not getting what you want, getting what you don't want, birth, aging, death, sickness.

23
00:02:49,462 --> 00:02:56,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And finally, it says, in short, the five assumed aggregates are suffering.

24
00:02:57,565 --> 00:02:57,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

25
00:02:59,509 --> 00:03:20,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, to some extent, know... like everybody experiences aging, everybody experiences loss, everybody experiences these things, but seeing... to the... seeing suffering to the full extent, meaning seeing the five assumed aggregates as sort of a phenomenon, that's already quite a...

26
00:03:21,323 --> 00:03:27,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite an achievement that would put you in the same position where you're able to see all the other ones as well.

27
00:03:28,689 --> 00:03:31,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... and, eh...

28
00:03:32,031 --> 00:03:39,457
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we discussed that in one of the previous talks where we said that, eh... there's a quote-unquote right type of suffering or suffering

29
00:03:41,784 --> 00:03:55,769
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what we're trying to get rid of, just out of habit, because we basically start with the default assumption that unpleasant feeling is what suffering is.

30
00:03:57,310 --> 00:04:05,053
[SPEAKER_04]: As a result of that, then, anything that manages or ameliorates your unpleasant feelings,

31
00:04:05,933 --> 00:04:15,396
[SPEAKER_00]: It might seem like it's an escape from suffering, but that's because the definition of what you're taking as suffering is already quite limited.

32
00:04:16,516 --> 00:04:21,538
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can already see how that's a good example of how these things are tied together.

33
00:04:23,178 --> 00:04:34,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in the case of craving, it has been clarified that if suffering is not a pleasant feeling, then what would craving be?

34
00:04:53,277 --> 00:04:58,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, so what is the standard definition of the origin of suffering, right?

35
00:04:58,747 --> 00:05:02,914
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is dhamma, which a patient should ask quite frequently.

36
00:05:03,515 --> 00:05:04,617
[SPEAKER_03]: What would you say about that?

37
00:05:16,413 --> 00:05:27,850
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, remember, it talks about craving for sensuality, craving for being or existence, and also craving for non-being or non-existence.

38
00:05:28,130 --> 00:05:31,756
[SPEAKER_03]: That is the, let's say, standard definition by the Buddha of what

39
00:05:39,073 --> 00:05:43,075
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how would you define, experientially, what craving is?

40
00:05:43,115 --> 00:05:49,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, usually, you, yeah, you read this, and it makes sense to everyone, to some extent, that, yeah, what craving is.

41
00:05:49,559 --> 00:05:55,182
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as it says there, if you haven't seen the cessation, merely on account of knowing what craving is,

42
00:06:04,611 --> 00:06:07,933
[SPEAKER_02]: negative feeling not being what suffering is.

43
00:06:08,593 --> 00:06:18,839
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that kind of approaching it from that angle, I would say craving is kind of what creates friction with negative feeling, you could say.

44
00:06:22,141 --> 00:06:27,424
[SPEAKER_02]: Craving is what kind of de facto turns negative feeling into suffering, I guess.

45
00:06:29,945 --> 00:06:31,706
[SPEAKER_02]: It makes it seem like suffering.

46
00:06:32,727 --> 00:06:35,189
[SPEAKER_02]: And is it just in relation to negative feeling?

47
00:06:35,950 --> 00:06:36,470
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no.

48
00:06:37,151 --> 00:06:42,095
[SPEAKER_02]: It would be in relation to the others, though.

49
00:06:47,299 --> 00:06:52,983
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I run the risk of walking into the same trap that Samādhi and the suttas walked into.

50
00:06:53,724 --> 00:06:57,567
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I was going to say, even when there is the pleasant feeling,

51
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:06,242
[SPEAKER_02]: The craving will still be in relation to a negative feeling, but that seems a bit dubious.

52
00:07:07,703 --> 00:07:21,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Kind of a more general negative feeling of, like, the undercutting, like, anxiety of, like, oh, I feel good now, but the anxiety of, will I be able to kind of maintain

53
00:07:24,812 --> 00:07:30,118
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, so even when there's pleasant feeling, there can still be the recognition that that's impermanent.

54
00:07:31,159 --> 00:07:32,120
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not going to last.

55
00:07:32,480 --> 00:07:38,106
[SPEAKER_03]: And even though that pleasant feeling endures, you still suffer on account of it not being permanent.

56
00:07:38,667 --> 00:07:42,871
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means that there is still suffering, even in the presence of pleasant feeling.

57
00:07:43,592 --> 00:08:00,031
[SPEAKER_03]: So then, the goal of the practice is not to artificially or through effort bring about pleasant feeling, but rather to know and to learn how to dwell disconnected from all feeling, including the pleasurable

58
00:08:03,452 --> 00:08:06,373
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the five assumed aggregates, in general, that are suffering.

59
00:08:06,993 --> 00:08:11,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And it doesn't matter whether that setup happens to be a pleasant setup.

60
00:08:11,834 --> 00:08:14,054
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the fact that it's the five aggregates.

61
00:08:16,475 --> 00:08:29,117
[SPEAKER_00]: The fact that, even when you tell yourself, you might sort of develop the attitude that it's not yours, but fundamentally, unless you're an arahant, there is ownership there.

62
00:08:29,217 --> 00:08:32,998
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just sort of deny it.

63
00:08:34,134 --> 00:08:44,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Through a view, you have to actually go the opposite direction and acknowledge that for as long as these things are there, you're liable to suffering.

64
00:08:44,561 --> 00:08:49,044
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as the five aggregates are assumed, which is the starting position.

65
00:08:49,184 --> 00:08:59,170
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have like a on and off switch, where sometimes you have like the purified five aggregates and sometimes not.

66
00:09:00,791 --> 00:09:03,013
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yeah, from that point of view, you could say that

67
00:09:05,555 --> 00:09:11,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's not necessarily something that's very profound or deep.

68
00:09:12,219 --> 00:09:16,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Almost every Buddhist would say, yes, I understand that craving is suffering.

69
00:09:16,642 --> 00:09:25,727
[SPEAKER_00]: But the trick lies there in, basically, whatever you take from the suttas, it's always going to be an interpretation.

70
00:09:26,748 --> 00:09:26,888
[SPEAKER_04]: But

71
00:09:28,268 --> 00:09:39,033
[SPEAKER_04]: What makes you really understand the Four Noble Truths is not when you have the same information that the suttas describe, because anybody can have that.

72
00:09:39,573 --> 00:09:44,915
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when that interpretation that you have aligns with what the interpretation of the Buddha

73
00:09:47,263 --> 00:09:53,646
[SPEAKER_03]: In other words, that your understanding of suffering is actually the suffering the Buddha talks about, fully and completely.

74
00:09:53,866 --> 00:09:54,426
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

75
00:09:54,446 --> 00:09:56,907
[SPEAKER_03]: So that... For each and every noble truth, basically.

76
00:09:56,987 --> 00:10:06,511
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, that's why it's... it shouldn't be... practice shouldn't be done with the assumption that... that yes, okay, I already understood these things.

77
00:10:06,751 --> 00:10:10,072
[SPEAKER_00]: Now it's time to just do them and then I'll get to this result.

78
00:10:10,652 --> 00:10:17,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, it's by refining, revising and correcting your interpretation of what craving, if it be,

79
00:10:17,035 --> 00:10:20,718
[SPEAKER_00]: There even is, for example, that you would arrive at stream-entry.

80
00:10:26,839 --> 00:10:47,018
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I put the definition that I already have, not question it, and then I just do this practice on top of it, because that's really just operating kind of within the same understanding, within the same... even if you get some results out of it, you haven't, like, left the wrong view that you started with.

81
00:10:47,058 --> 00:10:49,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the Buddha said, there is wrong view,

82
00:10:50,901 --> 00:10:53,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you not understanding Four Noble Truths?

83
00:10:53,644 --> 00:11:01,490
[SPEAKER_04]: That results in wrong intention, that results in wrong speech, that results in wrong action, and it goes all the way even to wrong liberation.

84
00:11:02,311 --> 00:11:12,319
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, you can liberate yourself from a limited, ultimately inaccurate, craving.

85
00:11:13,220 --> 00:11:14,841
[SPEAKER_04]: Because your understanding of a craving was

86
00:11:19,621 --> 00:11:26,946
[SPEAKER_04]: When it comes to craving, as the Buddha said, defilements cannot be abandoned without knowing and seeing what those defilements are first.

87
00:11:27,627 --> 00:11:39,035
[SPEAKER_00]: He said if you don't see craving clearly, it's not like you can expect that just doing the practice will somehow address that thing that you don't clearly know.

88
00:11:39,775 --> 00:11:41,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's addressed by knowing what it is.

89
00:11:46,133 --> 00:11:50,098
[SPEAKER_03]: But when one sees one of these Noble Truths, you basically see them all.

90
00:11:50,799 --> 00:11:53,763
[SPEAKER_03]: But there's still an order to these Four Noble Truths.

91
00:11:54,685 --> 00:11:54,865
[SPEAKER_03]: And...

92
00:11:55,523 --> 00:12:01,044
[SPEAKER_03]: The Noble Eightfold Path, which is what people generally take to be the practice, is actually the last one.

