139
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:15,784
[SPEAKER_04]: You expose yourself to the suffering by just not acting out of your desires.

140
00:17:15,924 --> 00:17:22,650
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean you need to go all... let go the whole way and actually force yourself

141
00:17:24,847 --> 00:17:26,808
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like, you know, sleeping on a bed of nails.

142
00:17:27,489 --> 00:17:27,989
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

143
00:17:28,129 --> 00:17:37,376
[SPEAKER_04]: When there's greed, when your mind is greedy, when it's averse, you don't act out of those things, and the right suffering, as we talked about, will be there.

144
00:17:37,916 --> 00:17:42,039
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's your time to start reflecting on

145
00:17:44,843 --> 00:17:45,303
[SPEAKER_04]: How do I get rid of it?

146
00:17:45,743 --> 00:17:57,668
[SPEAKER_04]: How do I escape this suffering in that timeless way that doesn't require me to go through this process of explaining it, rationalizing it, or sort of... Psychologizing.

147
00:17:57,688 --> 00:18:01,569
[SPEAKER_04]: Psychologizing, or doing something to get rid of it.

148
00:18:01,609 --> 00:18:01,949
[SPEAKER_04]: But no.

149
00:18:02,370 --> 00:18:07,151
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I directly see in that timeless manner what is the cessation of that suffering.

150
00:18:07,171 --> 00:18:10,893
[SPEAKER_04]: As the Buddha said, the liberation by mind and liberation by wisdom

151
00:18:11,493 --> 00:18:14,314
[SPEAKER_04]: Where those unwholesome states cease with that remainder.

152
00:18:15,274 --> 00:18:25,896
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that should really be the criteria for, like, to be seeing from the start.

153
00:18:25,956 --> 00:18:32,337
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why it's kind of painful, because it's more pleasant to just assume that, yes, okay, I got all this right.

154
00:18:32,477 --> 00:18:33,797
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, I just need to do this practice.

155
00:18:33,937 --> 00:18:38,378
[SPEAKER_04]: But going in that sort of more personal way to, like,

156
00:18:43,193 --> 00:18:44,094
[SPEAKER_04]: into your own views.

157
00:18:45,415 --> 00:18:49,758
[SPEAKER_04]: That part is... that's why you cannot do it without faith.

158
00:18:49,818 --> 00:18:57,843
[SPEAKER_03]: Essentially, your personality is inextricably bound up with personality view, so you can't separate them.

159
00:18:58,284 --> 00:19:10,252
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you go and dig deeper, then at some point, it is those personality view or views that will come up for question, which experientially means that you bring yourself up for question.

160
00:19:11,356 --> 00:19:13,278
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

161
00:19:13,358 --> 00:19:14,579
[SPEAKER_04]: Ignorance wants to survive.

162
00:19:14,839 --> 00:19:23,946
[SPEAKER_00]: And, obviously, if enlightenment were easy, you know, you wouldn't require a Buddha to sort of discover the way out.

163
00:19:23,966 --> 00:19:26,168
[SPEAKER_00]: People would naturally be taking that direction.

164
00:19:26,228 --> 00:19:28,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as he says, it's always against the grain.

165
00:19:29,271 --> 00:19:32,994
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, even when you're trying to find the way out of craving,

166
00:19:33,734 --> 00:19:43,877
[SPEAKER_04]: You can easily slip into these attitudes of, like I said before, trying to get rid of things, trying to manage your feelings, even though to you it feels like you are practicing to overcome craving.

167
00:19:44,377 --> 00:19:50,778
